PMID- 41337365
OWN - NLM
STAT- MEDLINE
DCOM- 20251203
LR  - 20251203
IS  - 2694-0604 (Electronic)
IS  - 2375-7477 (Linking)
VI  - 2025
DP  - 2025 Jul
TI  - Decoupled Representation Learning for Difference Medical Report Generation.
PG  - 1-4
LID - 10.1109/EMBC58623.2025.11252715 [doi]
AB  - Medical report generation is crucial for assessing disease progression and 
      treatment response, as radiologists compare current and prior images to identify 
      critical changes. Inspired by this practice, we introduce Difference Medical 
      Report Generation (DiffMRG), a task that generates detailed descriptions of image 
      differences to enhance disease monitoring and clinical decision-making. To 
      address this task, we propose D(2)MRG (Decoupled Representation guided Difference 
      Medical Report Generation), a framework specifically designed for Chest X-ray 
      images. Our approach is motivated by the way radiologists first identify 
      corresponding regions before detecting differences. To mimic this process, we 
      develop a decoupled reconstruction network that bidirectionally disentangles 
      changed and unchanged representations through cross-reconstruction supervision. 
      These decoupled features are then used for an initial binary classification to 
      determine the presence of changes. If differences exist, we leverage a change 
      prompt and the extracted representations to generate comprehensive reports using 
      a pre-trained large language model (LLM). To facilitate research in DiffMRG, we 
      construct Med-Diff, a large-scale dataset containing 82,039 paired Chest X-ray 
      images with corresponding reports. Extensive experiments on Med-Diff demonstrate 
      that D(2)MRG significantly outperforms state-of-the-art image difference 
      captioning methods, effectively capturing subtle yet critical medical 
      changes.Clinical Relevance-Automating the identification of differences between 
      medical images can assist in disease monitoring and treatment evaluation. Our 
      framework enables precise detection of pathological changes in Chest X-rays by 
      disentangling changed and unchanged representations, aligning with radiologists' 
      diagnostic process. This approach aids clinical practitioners in assessing 
      disease progression and treatment efficacy, enhancing decision-making in medical 
      imaging analysis.
