OAB - Heart failure (HF) is a leading cause of death worldwide. Early identification of 
      individuals at high risk could facilitate interventions to slow disease 
      progression. In this study, we develop an approach to improve HF risk prediction 
      by combining patient genetic information and clinical information from electronic 
      health records (EHR). We create two risk scores: a polygenic risk score (PRS) 
      based on genetic information, and a clinical risk score (ClinRS) based on patient 
      EHR. We test how well these scores predict HF before diagnosis. Both PRS and 
      ClinRS improve predictions individually and identify high-risk individuals up to 
      eight years in advance. When used together, they provide greater accuracy, 
      predicting HF up to ten years before diagnosis. We suggest that combining genetic 
      and clinical information could help doctors detect HF earlier for better 
      treatment and prevention strategies in the future.
