DP  - 2025
TI  - Clinical Sentiment Analysis by Large Language Models Enhances Prediction of 
      Hepatorenal Syndrome.
PG  - 100797
LID - 10.1016/j.gastha.2025.100797 [doi]
LID - 100797
AB  - BACKGROUND AND AIMS: Hepatorenal syndrome-acute kidney injury (HRS-AKI) is a 
      severe complication of decompensated cirrhosis that is challenging to predict. 
      Large language models are robust for natural language processing (NLP). We aimed 
      to determine if clinical sentiment in unstructured text can be derived by large 
      language models and used to improve prediction of HRS-AKI over traditional 
      predictors alone. METHODS: Cohort study consisting of adult patients admitted to 
      a single academic medical center with decompensated cirrhosis and AKI. We used 
      Microsoft Azure OpenAI generative pretrained transformer (GPT)-4o to derive a 
      sentiment score ranging from 0 to 1 for HRS-AKI and conduct NLP extraction of 
      clinical terms associated with HRS-AKI in admission notes. The area under the 
      receiver operator curve (AUROC) was compared in logistic regression models 
      incorporating structured variables with versus without sentiment scores and 
      NLP-extracted clinical terms. RESULTS: In our cohort of 314 participants, the 
      median age was 60, 48% were female, and the median Model for End Stage Liver 
      Disease (MELD) was 31. Higher sentiment score was associated with the diagnosis 
      of HRS-AKI (odds ratio: 1.52 per 0.1; 95% confidence interval: 1.12-2.14) in 
      multivariate models. AUROC using structured clinical covariates alone was 0.63. 
      With the addition of GPT-4o-derived sentiment score and clinical terms to 
      structured covariates, the final model yielded an improved AUROC of 0.79 (P < 
      .01). CONCLUSION: Clinical texts contain data that are difficult to extract using 
      standard methodologies. Sentiment analysis and NLP-based variable derivation with 
      GPT-4o in clinical application is feasible and can improve the prediction of 
      HRS-AKI over structured data alone.
