TI  - Precision in Parsing: Evaluation of an Open-Source Named Entity Recognizer (NER) 
      in Veterinary Oncology.
PG  - 102-108
LID - 10.1111/vco.13035 [doi]
AB  - Integrating Artificial Intelligence (AI) through Natural Language Processing 
      (NLP) can improve veterinary medical oncology clinical record analytics. Named 
      Entity Recognition (NER), a critical component of NLP, can facilitate efficient 
      data extraction and automated labelling for research and clinical 
      decision-making. This study assesses the efficacy of the Bio-Epidemiology-NER 
      (BioEN), an open-source NER developed using human epidemiological and medical 
      data, on veterinary medical oncology records. The NER's performance was compared 
      with manual annotations by a veterinary medical oncologist and a veterinary 
      intern. Evaluation metrics included Jaccard similarity, intra-rater reliability, 
      ROUGE scores, and standard NER performance metrics (precision, recall, F1-score). 
      Results indicate poor direct translatability to veterinary medical oncology 
      record text and room for improvement in the NER's performance, with precision, 
      recall, and F1-score suggesting a marginally better alignment with the oncologist 
      than the intern. While challenges remain, these insights contribute to the 
      ongoing development of AI tools tailored for veterinary healthcare and highlight 
      the need for veterinary-specific models.
