DP  - 2022 Nov 1
TI  - Ethical Implications of the Use of Language Analysis Technologies for the 
      Diagnosis and Prediction of Psychiatric Disorders.
PG  - e41014
LID - 10.2196/41014 [doi]
LID - e41014
AB  - Recent developments in artificial intelligence technologies have come to a point 
      where machine learning algorithms can infer mental status based on someone's 
      photos and texts posted on social media. More than that, these algorithms are 
      able to predict, with a reasonable degree of accuracy, future mental illness. 
      They potentially represent an important advance in mental health care for 
      preventive and early diagnosis initiatives, and for aiding professionals in the 
      follow-up and prognosis of their patients. However, important issues call for 
      major caution in the use of such technologies, namely, privacy and the stigma 
      related to mental disorders. In this paper, we discuss the bioethical 
      implications of using such technologies to diagnose and predict future mental 
      illness, given the current scenario of swiftly growing technologies that analyze 
      human language and the online availability of personal information given by 
      social media. We also suggest future directions to be taken to minimize the 
      misuse of such important technologies.
