TI  - ReHouSED: A novel measurement of Veteran housing stability using natural language 
      processing.
PG  - 103903
LID - S1532-0464(21)00232-X [pii]
LID - 10.1016/j.jbi.2021.103903 [doi]
AB  - Housing stability is an important determinant of health. The US Department of 
      Veterans Affairs (VA) administers several programs to assist Veterans 
      experiencing unstable housing. Measuring long-term housing stability of Veterans 
      who receive assistance from VA is difficult due to a lack of standardized 
      structured documentation in the Electronic Health Record (EHR). However, the text 
      of clinical notes often contains detailed information about Veterans' housing 
      situations that may be extracted using natural language processing (NLP). We 
      present a novel NLP-based measurement of Veteran housing stability: Relative 
      Housing Stability in Electronic Documentation (ReHouSED). We first develop and 
      evaluate a system for classifying documents containing information about 
      Veterans' housing situations. Next, we aggregate information from multiple 
      documents to derive a patient-level measurement of housing stability. Finally, we 
      demonstrate this method's ability to differentiate between Veterans who are 
      stably and unstably housed. Thus, ReHouSED provides an important methodological 
      framework for the study of long-term housing stability among Veterans receiving 
      housing assistance.
