PMID- 24303281
OWN - NLM
STAT- PubMed-not-MEDLINE
DCOM- 20131204
LR  - 20200929
IS  - 2153-4063 (Print)
VI  - 2013
DP  - 2013
TI  - Automated tools for phenotype extraction from medical records.
PG  - 283
AB  - Clinical research studying critical illness phenotypes relies on the 
      identification of clinical syndromes defined by consensus definitions. 
      Historically, identifying phenotypes has required manual chart review, a time and 
      resource intensive process. The overall research goal of C ritical I llness PH 
      enotype E xt R action (deCIPHER) project is to develop automated approaches based 
      on natural language processing and machine learning that accurately identify 
      phenotypes from EMR. We chose pneumonia as our first critical illness phenotype 
      and conducted preliminary experiments to explore the problem space. In this 
      abstract, we outline the tools we built for processing clinical records, present 
      our preliminary findings for pneumonia extraction, and describe future steps.
