VI  - 15
IP  - 1
DP  - 2008 Jan-Feb
TI  - Automated acquisition of disease drug knowledge from biomedical and clinical 
      documents: an initial study.
PG  - 87-98
AB  - OBJECTIVE: Explore the automated acquisition of knowledge in biomedical and 
      clinical documents using text mining and statistical techniques to identify 
      disease-drug associations. DESIGN: Biomedical literature and clinical narratives 
      from the patient record were mined to gather knowledge about disease-drug 
      associations. Two NLP systems, BioMedLEE and MedLEE, were applied to Medline 
      articles and discharge summaries, respectively. Disease and drug entities were 
      identified using the NLP systems in addition to MeSH annotations for the Medline 
      articles. Focusing on eight diseases, co-occurrence statistics were applied to 
      compute and evaluate the strength of association between each disease and 
      relevant drugs. RESULTS: Ranked lists of disease-drug pairs were generated and 
      cutoffs calculated for identifying stronger associations among these pairs for 
      further analysis. Differences and similarities between the text sources (i.e., 
      biomedical literature and patient record) and annotations (i.e., MeSH and 
      NLP-extracted UMLS concepts) with regards to disease-drug knowledge were 
      observed. CONCLUSION: This paper presents a method for acquiring disease-specific 
      knowledge and a feasibility study of the method. The method is based on applying 
      a combination of NLP and statistical techniques to both biomedical and clinical 
      documents. The approach enabled extraction of knowledge about the drugs 
      clinicians are using for patients with specific diseases based on the patient 
      record, while it is also acquired knowledge of drugs frequently involved in 
      controlled trials for those same diseases. In comparing the disease-drug 
      associations, we found the results to be appropriate: the two text sources 
      contained consistent as well as complementary knowledge, and manual review of the 
      top five disease-drug associations by a medical expert supported their 
      correctness across the diseases.
